at look  at games as something that they want to  entertain themselves with how they want  to spend their time and then when you  look at the very high fidelity things  that we're able to put on their  television screens to the console  industry and the power of today's  console I think it's the the amount of  game content out there the amount of  gamers  probably have more gamers than we've  ever seen playing all kinds of games  it's just a good thing for the console  business I think that's playing out  right now we have a good lineup of  free-to-play games on 360 and I think  it's an interesting business model and  creative mechanic for getting people to  play and things like happy Wars and  World of Tanks and we're face and stuff  on 360 I've done really well early on in  a new generation like we are with with  Xbox one you're free to play as a model  really requires a lot of people that's  how the whole model works because you're  going to monetize such a smaller set  we've embraced free-to-play long ago in  the console space and will continue to  do that I think it's an interesting form  of gaming and I see a lot of innovation  there and something that I think it will  be here to stay you talk about the  multiple price points in the multiple  ways that we monetize games today I  think that's a great thing for our  industry it's not that everybody has to  pay $60 to go play games you can buy  downloadable games that are cheaper  they're free to play games out there on  other devices you see advertising funded  games and things this is just great the  kind of top of the top of the top from a  fidelity standpoint from a completeness  at launch standpoint or your $60 console  retail games and you know I think it's a  viable model and it's proving out to be  successful we've got publishers out  there having tremendous success we're  seeing great return from some of our  franchises that we're bringing back and  we're bringing them back as retail games  because there